WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.660
In the vertebrate neural tube, the cells are already neural.

2
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.040
So what do the same parts do there?

3
00:00:09.983 --> 00:00:13.503
Here, proneural factors push a progenitor to become a neuron.

4
00:00:14.563 --> 00:00:16.583
Notch holds its neighbours back as progenitors.

5
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:21.113
The vertebrate relatives of Enhancer of split are the Hes genes.

6
00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:23.533
Hes inhibits proneural genes.

7
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:30.723
Progenitors make Notch, its ligand Dll1, the repressors Hes1 and Hes5, and proneural

8
00:00:30.723 --> 00:00:31.723
factors.

9
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:35.843
A cell that starts to differentiate raises Dll1.

10
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:39.323
That keeps the progenitors around it as progenitors.

11
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:42.293
Neurons first appear spaced apart.

12
00:00:43.273 --> 00:00:46.353
New ones arise in the gaps, and cells leave the pool over days.

13
00:00:47.417 --> 00:00:52.017
So lateral inhibition here is a brake on how fast progenitors are used up.

14
00:00:52.737 --> 00:00:56.777
A cell held back stays a dividing progenitor, and can compete again later.

15
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.680
Is Notch in the stem cell or the non-stem cell?

16
00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.580
Both cells have the receptor.

17
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:07.763
The cell whose Notch is more strongly activated stays a progenitor.

18
00:01:08.643 --> 00:01:11.103
The cell that keeps sending more Delta differentiates.

19
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:16.693
Mice lacking Hes1 make neurons too early, and have severe neural tube defects.

20
00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:21.627
Remove Hes5 as well, and progenitors are lost still faster.

21
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:24.147
a surprise about timing.

22
00:01:25.047 --> 00:01:28.927
In cultured cells, Hes1 rises and falls about every two hours.

23
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.980
Hes1 represses its own gene.

24
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.940
Its RNA and protein are short-lived, and making new protein takes time.

25
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:41.663
In mouse neural progenitors, Hes1 oscillates too.

26
00:01:41.663 --> 00:01:45.493
Neurog2 and Dll1 cycle in the opposite phase.

27
00:01:46.517 --> 00:01:51.157
In cells becoming neurons, Hes1 falls, and Neurog2 stays high.

28
00:01:51.937 --> 00:01:54.177
So a brief pulse of Delta is not a commitment.

29
00:01:55.300 --> 00:02:01.580
Predict. In cultured progenitors, use light to hold Ascl1 steady, or make it

30
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.800
oscillate. Which become neurons?

31
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.320
Steady Ascl1 pushed cells to become neurons.

32
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.740
Oscillating Ascl1 kept them dividing as progenitors.

33
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.220
Last question. Does a proneural factor also decide what kind of neuron forms?

34
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:29.693
In the mouse forebrain, Neurog2 is high in dorsal progenitors.

35
00:02:30.693 --> 00:02:32.653
Ascl1 is high in ventral ones.

36
00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:36.467
Researchers swapped them, each into the other's place.

37
00:02:38.847 --> 00:02:41.307
Does the factor carry the identity, or the territory?

38
00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:52.767
Neither, fully. Ascl1 pushed dorsal cells partly ventral.

39
00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.940
In the ventral forebrain, Neurog2 restored neurons, but did not push them dorsal.

40
00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.380
The swaps were not mirror images.

41
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.990
In vertebrates, the loop is a brake and a clock.

42
00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.210
The factor carries part of the identity; the tissue and the cell's history shape the

43
00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:10.470
rest.
